A||N||3||Seanh||Need new internals for rear calipers?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||04-21-2001||10:19 PM||||I swear, this is my last topic about my damn rear brakes. Promise.<BR>Anyway, Looking at the fiero store website, they have 2 different rebuild kits for the rear calipers. One ($48.25) included the new internal parts (piston, springs, seals, boots, etc.). The other ($12.02) only includes the seals and boots. Which one do I need? Do I really need to opt for the more expensive one with the new internals, or keep the current spring and piston I have in now? I would normally just buy the one with new internals without question, but I am so strapped for money now every little bit counts. So, do I really need the more expensive kit? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||04-21-2001||11:01 PM||||Sean, the only way you will know what you need is to take your old calipers apart and examine the parts.  I would suggest the following....<P>1. Buy the inexpensive kit.  I got mine from carparts for about $4. Don't open it until you have disassembled and examined you old caliper (including loosening the bleeder).<P>2.  Find an AutoZone.  They sell rebuilt rear calipers for something like $36 plus $80 core deposit.  No that is not a typo....they REALLY want your old core in return!<P>If your old caliper is badly pitted, bleeder breaks off, etc., buy the rebuilt caliper and save or return the kit.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||04-21-2001||11:06 PM||||Holy Crap! Rebuild rear calipers for $36??? I will take 6! I check NAPA and Advance Auto (only 2 in my Hick Town) and they were nothing short of $70 each, up to $130. I might just say screw it, and take the car down for this week and do it. Id have to drive my Bronco, but its pretty dang nice for a Ford, except the 8 mpg thats a killer with the gas prices. I'm gonna rip em off tonight.<P>Oh yeah, my bleeder screws work perfetly! Very suprising, but they do. My stock ebrake is also in perfect working condition. I don't want to have any future problems, so I'm gonna redo my calipers now. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-21-2001||11:20 PM||||Are you sure you even need to mess with rebuilding??????<P>You normally would only rebuild a caliper if it isn't working right. This is NOT required for ever pad change.<P>If a caliper is draging it can just as easily be the slide bolts or the inside pad binding against the caliper support bracket. I would check those out carefully before rebuilding....<P>Also read the other thread you have. I had other comments about the recall there.||||reg||1
